Prove representation independence for the permitted clients (@computing-abstraction-refinement: 0, is #chapter, #hephaestus-core "Protect-representation-assumptions-across-construction-and-observation.lingua#r_00CC9AF3F2F10A112C61003F3E") { r_008631FFAC6274EB0CF62F4E8E
Relate a valid tuple state t to a valid mask state b exactly when their abstractions are equal, including the same universe width. To show that one implementation can replace the other for the defined interface, prove three obligations: constructors establish related states; observers return equal abstract observations; and each transforming operation maps related states to related successors while preserving old values. Invalid ordinary inputs must have the same specified failure class and effects.

For the empty constructor both abstractions are (m,∅). For contains(x), tuple membership is equivalent to a set bit at x by the relation. Both members observers return the unique increasing enumeration of that set, and extent returns the same m. Insertion establishes S∪{x} on both sides; removal establishes S∖{x}. The preceding invariant argument ensures each result remains a permitted representation. Exact type/range checks reject the same invalid scalar inputs. `from_values` follows by induction over its finite validated tuple, with the accumulating abstraction equal to the set of the processed prefix.

Now consider a client making finitely many permitted calls, possibly choosing the next call from earlier permitted observations. Initially its corresponding values are related. At each step equal observations lead the same deterministic client to the same next call and arguments; the operation obligations then preserve equal observations and related stored values. Induction gives the same complete permitted observation trace. Earlier aliases remain related because the operations preserve old abstract values. This is an all-valid-state argument under the stated execution model, not a claim that testing a few histories proves arbitrary software equivalent.

The claim deliberately excludes observing type names, object identity, private fields, default repr, timing or allocation failure. A client that asks whether an insertion returned the identical object can distinguish the implementations: TupleSlots reuses self for an already present slot, while MaskSlots constructs a value. That does not violate their stated abstract contract. If identity or a time bound becomes a required observation, the proof obligations change; exclusion cannot then be used to claim the larger behavior is preserved.

An implementation relation need not be equality of layouts or one-to-one for every design. A different representation could admit several encodings for one value, provided all permitted operations respect that equivalence. Our canonical encodings make the proof simple but are not the definition of abstraction. A cached value or internal rearrangement can preserve α while changing the representation; exceptions, races and exposed references may make that rearrangement observable and need separate analysis.

**Work:** complete the observer and transformer proofs without calling the two classes equivalent merely because they have matching method names. Give a one-call distinguishing client outside the contract, and explain how to broaden the contract responsibly. Relate this local refinement argument to [Prove the remote ledger refines a serial prefix](Prove-the-remote-ledger-refines-a-serial-prefix.lingua#r_00DCF55733BDF283803289BC1C) while retaining the different assumptions about persistent state, communication and allowed traces.

